@page "/sms"
@attribute [AllowAnonymous]

<PageTitle>Text message consent</PageTitle>

<div class="page-head">
    <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Medical Manager</p>
        <h1>Text message consent</h1>
        <p class="muted">This page is public at <a href="@SmsCopy.ConsentUrl">@SmsCopy.ConsentUrl</a>. You can read it without an account. Medical Manager is live.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<article class="legal-page">
    <p>
        <strong>Medical Manager</strong> is a service of @SmsCopy.BusinessName.
        Support email: <a href="mailto:@SmsCopy.SupportEmail">@SmsCopy.SupportEmail</a>.
        The program name is Medical Manager.
    </p>

    <h2>How you opt in</h2>
    <p>
        A patient opts in on their own Profile after signing in. The patient types their own mobile number and checks a box that is unchecked until they check it.
        Saving a phone number without that box does not opt anyone in.
        A doctor cannot type the patient's phone number, and a doctor never sees the approval code.
        Medical Manager then texts only the number the patient saved and consented to.
    </p>
    <p>On Profile, the box looks like this and starts unchecked:</p>
    <blockquote class="sms-consent-quote">
        <input type="checkbox" disabled aria-disabled="true" />
        <SmsConsentLabel />
    </blockquote>
    <p>
        The box on this public page is a picture of that Profile control. Checking it here does not opt you in.
        Opt-in happens when a signed-in patient checks the same box on Profile and presses Save details.
        If the patient changes the mobile number, the box is cleared and they have to check it again.
        Medical Manager stores the time of that consent and the number it covers.
    </p>

    <h2>What texts are sent</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Chart-access approval code.</strong>
            When a doctor asks to open that patient's records, Medical Manager texts a one-time six-digit approval code to the consented number.
            The code expires in 15 minutes. The doctor does not receive the code.
            No approval text is sent unless consent for that number is on file.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Appointment reminder.</strong>
            One text the day before a visit, and only while appointment reminders stay on and consent is on file.
            Medication reminders and to-do reminders are phone calls. They are not text messages.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Monitor Share.</strong>
            A separate opt-in. A signed-in account holder opens Monitor, chooses Share, types a mobile number, and presses Send.
            One text goes to that number with a family-monitor link and a four-digit passcode.
            No Monitor Share text is sent unless Send is pressed.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Message frequency varies.
        An approval code is sent when a doctor requests access.
        An appointment reminder sends at most one text on the day before each visit.
        Monitor Share sends one text each time Send is pressed.
        Message and data rates may apply.
    </p>
    <p>
        Reply STOP to opt out. After STOP, you receive one confirmation and then no further texts from that number.
        Reply START to allow texts again.
        Reply HELP for help, or email <a href="mailto:@SmsCopy.SupportEmail">@SmsCopy.SupportEmail</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
        Read the <a href="@SmsCopy.PrivacyUrl">Privacy Policy</a> and the <a href="@SmsCopy.TermsUrl">Terms and Conditions</a>.
        No mobile information will be shared with third parties or affiliates for marketing or promotional purposes.
        Text messaging originator opt-in data and consent will not be shared with any third parties.
        Medical Manager does not sell message data.
    </p>
</article>
